A||||1||VISCERAL||GRAND AM BRAKES-possible park/brk solution?
Z||000000||VISCERAL||06-17-99||11:04 AM||||Looking at the JC Whitney catalog and I<br>stumbled upon an in line hydraulic brake lock. What do you guys think as a solution <br>for grand am brake converters with no parking<br>hand brake?
Z||000001||theogre||06-17-99||06:54 PM||||Line locks are nothing new. I've used them for many years. Mostly on wreckers where it's often nessisary to lock all the wheels up when winching. They are also use in drag racing to lock the front wheels durring burnout in many classes. (You are not allowed to burn DOT tires anywhere but the pit on most tracks. It tears up the track.)<p>Line locks work by enabling a check valve in the break line. You flip the lever or pull the knob and then press the break peddal. The presure then is held by the lock until the user releases it.<p>The problem with using a line lock for a parking break is that the break system is not designed to cary a sustained presure for long periods of time. Usually the first thing to start leaking are the piston seals but other things can fail too. When used on a wrecker, we only use it while actually working, we don't use it to replace a hand break. We use it to prevent winch loads from being transmitted to the drive train which could break things.<p>Another posible issue with elimination of the parking/emergency brake is legal liability. If the hydraulics fail, the  cable system is capable of stopping the car, altho stopping distance will be incrediblly longer. Also if there is a problem with "upgraded" brakes that eliminate a function, your insurance company could refuse to cover any damage/injury you cause. You could also face criminal charges in many states as well.
